    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so if the overtones are what create that \"color\", are there certain frequencies or types of overtones that can make a flute sound a bit \"ghostly\" or less pure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the specific makeup of the overtones dictates the \"color,\" and certain harmonic characteristics can certainly lend a ghostly or less pure quality to a flute's sound. If the overtones are overly complex, dissonant, or contain a lot of high-frequency noise, it can sound less pure.\n\nA \"ghostly\" quality often arises when there is a significant presence of inharmonicity—meaning the overtones aren't perfectly integer multiples of the fundamental frequency. This slight \"out-of-tune-ness\" in the higher partials creates a shimmering, slightly unstable texture, which can sound ethereal or spectral. Conversely, a very clean, well-tuned instrument will have a more predictable, consonant overtone structure.",
      "source": "target",
